\paragraph{Lead-lag analysis.}
At $\alpha{=}1.0$, token entropy Granger-precedes distinct-3 at the same consensus level as distinct-1 and distinct-2: $K{\geq}4/5$ methods, $S{\geq}3/4$ seeds (Tier~3).
However, the optimal lag is less stable than for distinct-1/2: at max\_lag${=}7$, the lag standard deviation across seeds exceeds 1.5 generations, compared to $<0.5$ for distinct-1/2.
This instability reflects bimodal lag distributions (modes at lag~1 and lag~3--4), suggesting that distinct-3 captures two temporally distinct diversity loss mechanisms: rapid n-gram homogenization (lag~1) and slower distributional narrowing (lag~3--4).

\paragraph{Sensitivity to sequence length.}
Higher-order $n$-gram diversity metrics are more sensitive to generation length and random sampling variability.
With our 512-token generation length, distinct-3 has a smaller effective denominator (number of possible unique trigrams observed) than distinct-1 or distinct-2, amplifying stochastic fluctuations.
This partially explains the higher lag instability: the signal-to-noise ratio for temporal precedence is lower for distinct-3, requiring more observations (longer $T$ or more seeds) to achieve stable estimates.
For this reason, we report distinct-3 in this appendix rather than in the main text, where distinct-1 and distinct-2 provide cleaner evidence of the entropy-leads-diversity pattern.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Model collapse and canary metrics.}
Iterative training collapse was formalized by \citet{shumailov2024curse} and \citet{alemohammad2024mad}, who showed that recursively replacing real data with model-generated text causes progressive tail erosion; \citet{schaeffer2025position} systematize eight distinct notions of ``collapse'' that this literature conflates.
Theoretical analyses have sharpened these results: \citet{dohmatob2024tale} recast collapse as a change in scaling-law exponents, \citet{dohmatob2025strong} proved that collapse can occur even at vanishingly small synthetic fractions, \citet{seddik2025statistical} provide a statistical analysis of iterative contamination dynamics, and \citet{marchi2025challenging} challenge the universality of collapse under diverse training conditions.
Several works have proposed monitoring metrics: \citet{du2026geometric} observe that calibration error rises before accuracy degrades, \citet{du2025scales} report ECE increases at 30\% contamination before accuracy drops, \citet{gambetta2024surplexity} introduces surplexity as a collapse detector, and \citet{drayson2025detection} show that machine-generated text detection can prevent collapse, and \citet{li2025verification} propose verification-based mitigation.
We differ from the closest predecessor, \citet{du2026geometric}, in four respects: (i)~we compare an entire family of canary metrics; (ii)~we cross-validate across five statistical methods; (iii)~we map detectability as a function of contamination regime; and (iv)~we treat method-selection sensitivity itself as a primary finding.

\paragraph{Data mixing phase transitions.}
\citet{kazdan2025collapse} map the conditions under which synthetic data triggers collapse versus improves performance, \citet{gu2024cmr} establish a scaling law for critical mixture ratios in continual pre-training, and \citet{gu2025datamixing} show that data mixing can induce phase transitions in knowledge acquisition---a direct theoretical analogue to our sharp detection onset, where a critical mixing ratio separates qualitatively different detection regimes.

\paragraph{Critical transitions and lead-lag methodology.}
\citet{scheffer2009early} established that systems approaching tipping points exhibit generic early-warning signals (rising autocorrelation and variance); \citet{dakos2012methods} developed a practical toolkit for estimating such signals, while \citet{boettiger2012limits} quantified detection limits in short time series---directly relevant to iterative training where $T{=}5$--$20$.
Crucially, \citet{truong2026phase} prove that entropy collapse in iterative training is a first-order (discontinuous) phase transition, structurally ruling out critical-slowing-down (CSD) early-warning signals; \citet{buffa2026entropy} independently propose token entropy as a monitoring metric.
Our ``detection onset'' is a \emph{statistical} threshold (PermG power crossing a detection floor), not a thermodynamic phase transition, and our cascade ordering describes within-generation temporal ordering of metrics, not CSD-type precursors.
The standard framework for testing temporal precedence is Granger causality~\cite{granger1969investigating}, with neural extensions for nonlinear settings~\citep{marcinkevics2022neural}; however, non-stationary series inflate rejection rates~\cite{granger1974spurious}, and the Toda--Yamamoto procedure~\cite{toda1995statistical} addresses this at reduced degrees of freedom severe in short series.
These methods are largely absent from the model collapse literature.

\paragraph{Analytical robustness.}
\citet{bell2022modeling} introduced multiverse analysis to ML, \citet{coretta2023multidimensional} demonstrated that 46 teams reached different conclusions on identical data, and \citet{kapoor2024reforms} synthesized these concerns into a consensus checklist.
Our work applies this perspective to model collapse: five methods across multiple contamination regimes produce conclusions ranging from universal significance to complete null.

\section*{Ethics Statement and AI Writing Disclosure}

\paragraph{AI writing assistance.}
This paper was drafted with assistance from Claude (Anthropic), used for LaTeX formatting, literature search, and iterative revision.
All scientific claims, experimental design decisions, data analysis, and interpretations are the sole responsibility of the authors.
The AI assistant did not design experiments, run code, or make editorial judgments about which results to include or exclude.

\paragraph{Responsible NLP considerations.}
This work studies degradation in iterative language model training.
We do not release models trained on synthetic data, as they exhibit reduced diversity and calibration.
Our findings highlight a failure mode relevant to the responsible deployment of language models: unmonitored iterative training on web-crawled data (which increasingly contains LLM outputs) may degrade model quality in ways detectable only through the multi-method framework we propose.
We encourage practitioners to implement canary monitoring in production training pipelines as a safeguard against silent quality degradation.
